0123_news_pile_check — NOT CARRIED AS A STEP, FOR `0105.noop`'S REASON VERBATIM.

The shared entry widens the closed set on `away_responders.piles` by one more member,
`ohmail/News`, the way Postgres allows: DROP CONSTRAINT then ADD CONSTRAINT, with the legacy
`ohmail/Reads` kept beside it because rows written before the 0.22 rename carry that spelling.
SQLite carries no CHECK for this column at all — it stores `piles` as a JSON text column, and the
closed set is refused at the write door instead (`request-apply.ts`, which reads `AWAY_PILES`
itself), so the sqlite widening IS the constant's new member and there is no table to rebuild.

The two sides carry different member counts on purpose. Postgres keeps BOTH spellings in the
CHECK, because rows written before the rename hold the legacy one and the constraint is checked
against what is stored. The device door carries ONE: `request-apply.ts` canonicalizes the spelling
BEFORE it asks the set, so a pre-0.22 install's ask is admitted and stored canonical.

WHAT CHANGES THE DAY THIS JOURNAL SHIPS is what `0102.noop` and `0104.noop` already say, and the
same answer serves this one.
